ot same diagonal. Diagonal through J10 NW-SE is I11, J10, K9 — so no.  \n\nActually, check diagonal NW-SE through J10:  \nI11(row11,colI), J10(row10,colJ), K9(row9,colK) — that’s three, plus H12 not on same line.  \n\nLet’s think simpler:  \nWhat about **M8**?  \nRow 8: H8=X, I8=O, J8=X, K8=., L8=O, M8=.  \nNot strong.  \n\nGiven the crowded center, best move is to **create an open three** or **make a straight four forcing a block then another threat**.  \nAfter checking, **L10** seems strongest:  \n- Makes straight four in row 10 (I10–L10).  \n- Also, diagonal from (F14=O, G13=O, H12=X, I11=X, J10=X, K9=X, L10=X, M9=O) — not five.  \n\nBut actually, if White doesn’t block M10, Black wins by playing M10 next for five in row 10. So White must block M10. Then Black can play elsewhere.  \n\nGiven the board, Black’s best immediate chance is **G9**? But G9 is empty and creates:  \nRow 9: G9=X, H9=O, I9=X, J9=X, K9=X, L9=X, M9=O — that’s X,O,X,X,X,X,O — no five.  \n\nWait, what about **F9**? Creates nothing.  \n\nLet’s check **M10**:  \nRow 10: I10–K10 are three X’s, M10 empty, N10 empty. If Black plays M10, then I10,J10,K10,M10 — not consecutive.  \n\nI think the winning move is **L10** because it forces White to respond at M10, and then Black has follow-up threats elsewhere (like diagonal H12–K9 plus L10).  \n\nBut is there a move that gives **two fours**?  \nTry **K8**:  \nRow 8: H8=X, I8=O, J8=X, K8=X, L8=O — only two X’s.  \nColumn K: K9=X, K10=X, K11=O, K8=X — not four.  \n\nActually, check **G11**:  \nDiagonal from G7=X, H8=X, I9=X, J10=X, K11=O — blocked.  \n\nGiven the constraints, **L10** seems the most forcing move to progress toward victory.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nL10 creates a straight four in row 10, forcing White to block at M10 immediately. This move also aligns with diagonal possibilities (J10–L10 not same diagonal though). It advances Black’s strongest row formation and keeps initiative. Other moves like M10 or K8 are less threatening.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{